* `pgToEF M ε : (T, r, θ, φ) ↦ (T + ε H_M(r), r, θ, φ)` and `pgFromEF M ε` are mutually inverse
  (globally), map `domEF` onto itself (`bijOn`), and are `C^∞` on `domEF`;
* `H_M' = 1/(1 + q)` with `q = √(2M/r)` (`hasDerivAt_HPG`), so the Jacobian of `pgToEF` is the
  explicit matrix `jac` with radial mixed entry `ε/(1 + q)` and `det jac = 1`; every entry is a
  genuine `HasDerivAt` (`hasDerivAt_pd_pgToEF`);
* pull-back identity `jacᵀ · (gEF ∘ pgToEF) · jac = gPG` on `domEF` (`pullback`);
* the same in the other direction (`pgFromEF`, `jacInv`, `pullback_inv`).

Bundled as `relation_toEF : CoordRelation (gPG M ε) (gEF M ε) domEF domEF …` and `relation_fromEF`.
At `r = 2M` (`M > 0`): `q = 1`, `H' = 1/2`, `jac 0 1 = ε/2`, `det = 1` — nothing degenerates
(`PGSpecial.horizon_data`). The primitive `H_M` is one choice; the literature's `T = t + …` differs
from `w − ε H_M` by an additive constant that depends on the normalisation of the EF time (with the
source convention `Schwarzschild.toEF` the constant is `0`; `mission/PHYSICS_BOUNDARY.md` E4), which
